Description: CVE-2026-54370 (+CVE-2026-54369 tooling/libacl): import upstream
 acl 2.4.0 symlink-safe rewrite onto Debian bullseye acl 2.2.53-10.
 .
 The acl 2.x utilities operated on attacker-controlled paths using an
 lstat() check followed by separate symlink-following syscalls.  An
 attacker who wins the race between check and use can swap a path
 component for a symlink and redirect a privileged getfacl/setfacl/chacl
 to an arbitrary file (CVE-2026-54370, CWE-367).  The same class of bug
 exists for pathname-based libacl callers (CVE-2026-54369); upstream
 keeps the legacy APIs symlink-following by design and adds new *_at
 variants for safe callers.
 .
 This patch brings the upstream 2.4.0 structural fix onto bullseye's
 2.2.53-10 post-quilt tree:
   * directory-fd walk_tree rewrite;
   * openat2(RESOLVE_NO_SYMLINKS) + fchmodat/xattrat compat shims;
   * public libacl *_at APIs (acl_get_file_at, acl_set_file_at,
     acl_delete_def_file_at, acl_extended_file_at) with ACL_1.3 / LT bump;
   * hardened getfacl / setfacl (including --restore -P) / chacl / do_set;
   * preserve Debian getfacl uninitialized-variable guard;
   * update sbits-restore / utf8-filenames / root-restore tests to use
     --physical with --restore (avoids unsafe-restore stderr warnings).
 .
 Package version in configure.ac is intentionally left at 2.2.53 for
 packaging consistency; only the openat2/xattrat/unsafe-restore feature
 probes from 2.4.0 are added.  The new public symbols require a
 debian/libacl1.symbols update at packaging time (outside this patch).
 .
 CVE-2026-54369 is covered by the same import (new *_at APIs + safe
 tool call sites).  The sibling CVE-2026-54369 hook is a no-op note so
 alphabetical apply order does not fight this patch.
